1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,340
This is RC at top speed, eight times slower than real life.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:08,735
Professional coaches judge a sprint stride at five key positions.

3
00:00:08,735 --> 00:00:19,081
We measured every one of them and compared him with world-class one-hundred-metre finalists and with college sprinters filmed the same way we filmed him.

4
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:21,871
Here are the five positions.

5
00:00:21,871 --> 00:00:24,972
Toe-off, when the back foot leaves the ground.

6
00:00:24,972 --> 00:00:25,916
Top of flight.

7
00:00:25,916 --> 00:00:27,804
Strike, just before landing.

8
00:00:27,804 --> 00:00:28,478
Touchdown.

9
00:00:28,478 --> 00:00:32,186
And full support, when his body is right over the foot.

10
00:00:33,083 --> 00:00:35,342
The biggest difference is at touchdown.

11
00:00:35,342 --> 00:00:38,123
Watch his right leg, in red, the one in the air.

12
00:00:38,123 --> 00:00:44,610
When his left foot lands, his right knee is still well behind his left knee, and the heel is back near his butt.

13
00:00:44,610 --> 00:00:48,781
Now the white figure: that's the world-class average, drawn to his size.

14
00:00:48,781 --> 00:00:54,921
Their swing knee has already come through, level with the other knee, with the heel tucked under the hips.

15
00:00:54,921 --> 00:00:56,890
Coaches call that the figure four.

16
00:00:56,890 --> 00:01:00,539
His thighs are thirty-four degrees apart; theirs are about ten.

17
00:01:01,417 --> 00:01:01,676
Why?

18
00:01:01,676 --> 00:01:03,035
It starts at toe-off.

19
00:01:03,035 --> 00:01:10,736
He pushes until his leg is completely straight, knee at about a hundred and seventy degrees and the hip far behind him.

20
00:01:10,736 --> 00:01:18,049
World-class sprinters leave the ground earlier, with the knee still bent, about a hundred and fifty-four degrees.

21
00:01:18,049 --> 00:01:22,579
Pushing all the way back costs time and leaves the leg further behind.

22
00:01:23,483 --> 00:01:27,005
Then, in the air, the thigh comes forward too slowly.

23
00:01:27,005 --> 00:01:28,467
Watch the yellow line.

24
00:01:28,467 --> 00:01:36,907
From toe-off to the next touchdown his thigh swings about thirty-four degrees; a world-class sprinter's swings about forty-two.

25
00:01:36,907 --> 00:01:40,827
Start further back, move slower, and the knee arrives late.

26
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:43,530
Two more numbers go with this.

27
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:49,877
Knee lift, how high the thigh comes up, is sixty-seven degrees; fast college sprinters reach seventy-six.

28
00:01:49,877 --> 00:01:56,646
And his thighs scissor at about four hundred and fifty degrees per second, where they reach almost five hundred.

29
00:01:56,646 --> 00:02:03,658
That speed is what predicts top speed among trained sprinters, and it takes strong, fast hip flexors and hamstrings.

30
00:02:03,658 --> 00:02:07,829
So strength matters, but to hit these positions, not instead of them.

31
00:02:08,717 --> 00:02:12,684
What's already at a professional level is the landing itself.

32
00:02:12,684 --> 00:02:23,350
His shin, his knee, and where the foot lands under his body all match the world-class numbers, and his ground contact is short and stiff, about a tenth of a second.

33
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:27,577
The first video was wrong to call touchdown distance the problem.

34
00:02:27,577 --> 00:02:28,813
It's the other leg.

35
00:02:29,717 --> 00:02:31,591
His arms show the same thing.

36
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:36,696
They swing a long way behind him, but in front the hand only reaches mid-chest.

37
00:02:36,696 --> 00:02:40,767
Let the hands come up to chin height as the knees come through.

38
00:02:41,683 --> 00:02:48,279
For the goal: twenty-one feet nine needs about nineteen point six miles an hour in the approach.

39
00:02:48,279 --> 00:02:58,515
His approach runs averaged eighteen point seven, so he needs about one more mile an hour, and a faster swing leg is part of how the step gets longer.

40
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:00,008
The plan.

41
00:03:00,008 --> 00:03:07,626
One: recover the leg earlier, with dribbles, A-skips checking the figure four, wall-drill switches, and wicket runs.

42
00:03:07,626 --> 00:03:10,909
Two: leave the ground sooner; push down, not back.

43
00:03:10,909 --> 00:03:17,805
Three: strength for the positions: knee drives, hanging knee raises, Nordic curls and Romanian deadlifts.

44
00:03:17,805 --> 00:03:22,862
Next session we'll measure the same positions again, plus jumps into the pit.
